      "thinking": "The logs point to unrelated ClientDisconnected errors rather than the real cause. The actual issue seems to be request volume surging 4.5x starting at 07:13, pushing CPU usage to its max of 3.0 cores while latency spikes dramatically across all percentiles. The oscillation in cpu_limit_cores between 3.0 and 6.0 suggests autoscaling is struggling to keep pace with the demand.\n\nThis confirms the throttling pattern: usage gets capped near 3 whenever the limit drops back down, classic autoscaling oscillation where the controller scales to 6, relieves pressure, drops to 3, then queues build again every few minutes. Request volume is climbing steadily too, but since cpu_limit_cores takes multiple oscillating values rather than one fixed value, this points to autoscaling-pathology rather than pure demand-saturation.\n\nChecking timestamps confirms it directly: every time cpu_limit_cores hits 6.0, p50 latency drops back to baseline ~44-46ms, and every time it drops to 3.0, latency spikes to 300-400ms. That's the clear signature of capacity oscillation driving the periodic latency spikes.\n\nRequest_volume also climbs from 1200 to 5400 and plateaus there, which could just be organic traffic growth prompting the scaling behavior in the first place. But since cpu_limit_cores itself takes multiple values across the window, that points squarely to autoscaling-pathology rather than demand-saturation \u2014 the capacity is oscillating, not just sitting fixed under load. I've already pulled the full metrics window and checked for changes, so nothing more there. I should also check the logs for any explicit scaling events to corroborate this.\n\nAlready reviewed the 07:00-07:32 logs and found no scaling entries. Checking service dependencies probably isn't needed since this looks like an internal autoscaling issue rather than a downstream dependency problem. I have enough to finalize the answer as autoscaling-pathology with high confidence.\n\n",
